docs/reports\P12-ARRIVALS.md:15:- The queue no longer depends on the frame rate. A game that is needed jumps the queue and is built at once. Once a game has arrived once on a machine, its next arrival costs 2 to 31 ms on the main thread instead of 16 to 618 ms (hockey 595 → 7 ms).
docs/reports\P12-ARRIVALS.md:21:**1. Main-thread cost of one arrival** (table in `TIERS.md`, section P12; all headless and busy). I measured it with a new probe, `engine/kernel/world/arrival_probe.gd`, which prepares on a worker and then loads on the main thread, as the queue does. Output must go to a file: a `print` into a pipe costs up to 15 ms a line and spoiled my first readings.
